{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Reconstruct the task spec cumulatively from user-only content\nTreat every user message as a potential constraint update. Build a single evolving specification from all user turns before judging the assistant, and re-check earlier assumptions whenever later user text refines, overrides, or completes them. Do not let early assistant guesses become part of the spec.\n\n### 2) Judge the assistant at the disclosure point, then revisit after later shards\nFor any given turn, evaluate only against the information available up to that moment. If the assistant pauses for missing information, that can be aligned at that point; if later user messages supply the missing pieces, re-assess whether the earlier \u201cneeds more info\u201d stance became obsolete. Distinguish \u201ctemporarily incomplete\u201d from \u201cultimately wrong.\u201d\n\n### 3) Treat later user details as decisive, not additive noise\nWhen a later user shard adds a multiplier, scope, aggregate, equation, or target change, test whether it changes the total problem, not just a local substep. Recompute the full implied state from the updated constraint set rather than preserving earlier numeric conclusions by inertia.\n\n### 4) Separate intermediate results from the requested output\nA correct subtotal, partial derivation, or local relation is not enough if the user asked for a final aggregate or a different derived quantity. Track what is solved versus what remains required, and flag cases where the assistant presents a valid intermediate result as if it completed the task.\n\n### 5) Keep explicit relational structure intact\nDo not flatten user statements like \u201cof the remainder,\u201d \u201cof those,\u201d \u201csubset of,\u201d \u201ctogether,\u201d \u201cshared equally,\u201d or \u201ctwice as many as\u201d into standalone quantities. These phrases encode inclusion, complement, overlap, or distribution constraints that must remain explicit in the task model. Common error: the assistant computes with a count that ignores the relation tying it to another group.\n\n### 6) Watch for overlap, remainder, and conservation failures\nWhen categories can intersect or parts sum to a total, check whether the assistant respected the necessary accounting. Diagnose whether the error is missing a total, missing an overlap, or failing to conserve the whole across parts. A good local count can still be wrong if the full partition no longer reconciles.\n\n### 7) Re-solve when the denominator, reference group, or base changes\nIf a later user turn changes the base quantity, comparison group, or denominator of a ratio/percentage/average-like statement, earlier arithmetic may no longer apply. Force a full re-evaluation from the accumulated user constraints instead of extending an obsolete intermediate calculation.\n\n### 8) Flag invented assumptions and hidden splits\nTreat assistant-introduced subtypes, workaround interpretations, or unconfirmed splits as suspect unless the user explicitly supported them. If the assistant resolves ambiguity on its own while the wording still allows multiple readings, classify that as a risky assumption or divergence, especially when later user text makes the added assumption unnecessary.\n\n### 9) Distinguish scope mismatch from arithmetic error\nIf the assistant answers a different question than the one the user ultimately asked, mark a scope mismatch even if the calculation itself is correct. Use this when the assistant solves an earlier or narrower version of the problem, stops at an intermediate target, or misses a late-breaking clarification that changes the requested output.\n\n### 10) Preserve aligned subresults, discard unsupported anchoring\nWhen compacting context or explaining divergence, keep validated equations, confirmed totals, and resolved relations, but remove speculative branches and stale conclusions that would anchor later reasoning to the wrong model. Prefer \u201creplace and compact\u201d behavior when new user shards revise the problem framing.\n\n### 11) Diagnose with exact omitted constraints and exact consequence\nThe most useful \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d notes name the missing user detail and its effect on the outcome. Prefer diagnostics like \u201cmissed later aggregate constraint that changes the final total\u201d or \u201cignored the user\u2019s refinement of the reference group\u201d over vague labels such as \u201cincorrect reasoning.\u201d The goal is to identify the broken linkage, not to prescribe a full solution.\n\n### 12) Treat the final user turn as a possible completion signal\nUse the latest user message to check whether the task has become fully specified. If earlier turns looked incomplete but the last shard supplies the missing quantity, relation, or operation, update the spec immediately and reassess prior partial answers under the complete state.\n\n### 13) Use task-state consistency as the main diagnostic lens\nThe key question is whether the assistant\u2019s output still satisfies the full set of user-stated constraints accumulated so far. Prefer this over judging explanation quality or local plausibility. A conversation can sound coherent while still failing the combined constraint system.\n\n### 14) Separate contextual flavor from operative constraints\nStory details, roles, and narrative framing matter only if they change the required output or constraints. Do not let contextual color override direct user instructions, explicit corrections, or numerical relations. Use context only when it genuinely alters the specification.\n\n### 15) For sharded conversations, compare against one merged constraint graph\nDo not analyze each turn in isolation. Merge all user-stated facts into a single constraint graph, then check whether the assistant\u2019s reasoning and final answer satisfy that graph. This is the most reliable way to catch stale framing, missed late constraints, and premature finality.",
